\paragraph{A cutoff test for degree-zero square-integrable cohomology.} Let $(M,g)$ be a connected complete Riemannian manifold with smooth or Lipschitz cutoffs $\chi_R$, equal to one on a radius-$R$ ball, compactly supported, and $|d\chi_R|\leq C/R$. Suppose a smooth function $u\in L^2(M)$ is harmonic. Multiplying $\Delta u=0$ by $\chi_R^2\overline u$, integrating by parts and taking real parts gives
\[
 \int\chi_R^2|du|^2
 \leq2\left(\int\chi_R^2|du|^2\right)^{1/2}
       \left(\int|d\chi_R|^2|u|^2\right)^{1/2}.
\]
Hence the left side is at most $4C^2R^{-2}\|u\|_2^2$. Letting $R\to\infty$ forces $du=0$, so $u$ is constant. If $M$ has infinite volume, square integrability then forces $u=0$.

\paragraph{What the argument would give for monopoles.} Once completeness, the cutoff control and infinite volume of a chosen strongly centered component are established, this excludes degree-zero $L^2$ harmonic forms there. It does not compute the middle-degree forms relevant to the source. For a general differential form, the analogous integration includes connection/curvature structure, and no sign forcing vanishing has been obtained here.

